jobs.description:
New Leaf Energy is an experienced clean energy developer that partners with landowners, policymakers, and other stakeholders to accelerate the transition to a world powered by renewable energy. We identify high-value sites, work with landowners and local communities to develop them, and partner with long-term asset owners to bring the most impactful clean energy projects to life.

New Leaf Energy's DG Capacity business, a new segment of the development business, develops distribution-connected standalone storage and solar-plus-storage projects to meet persistent capacity shortfalls driven by large loads such as data centers. Rather than relying on retail programs and tariffs paid for by ratepayers, DG Capacity portfolios will be aggregated, accredited, and sold into wholesale energy and capacity markets, and compensated directly or indirectly by large loads. The initial focus is PJM, where the need for capacity is most acute.

The DG Capacity team is small, senior, and cross-disciplinary, spanning grid solutions, interconnection and market participation, project sales and finance, and project development. It works closely with New Leaf's Regional Teams, the Utility-Scale team, and the Policy and Business Development team. The team is also architecting one of New Leaf Energy's DG development platforms of the future; the processes, tools, and product standards it builds are intended for adoption across markets as persistent capacity shortfalls emerge in other RTOs.

Unlike other development roles at New Leaf Energy that focus on the full range of development activities in established markets, this position is responsible primarily for developing beachhead positions in new markets and for guiding the transition of those markets from new, untested verticals into mature, core verticals with hundreds of MWs of opportunity. In this role you should have significant origination and project development experience and be excited about starting new markets, building your own playbook, and tackling the kinds of novel challenges that arise with first-of-their-kind opportunities in new geographies. The position will be fast-paced, will require superior organizational skills, and may require considerable travel, depending on where the opportunities arise.

The New Markets Project Developer will build and nurture relationships with local stakeholders: landowners, local governments, utilities, policy-makers, and others. You will be the primary regional expert for new markets in the company, and will need to quickly develop fundamental market knowledge and intelligence in new places to create and execute successful project development plans that will provide launchpads for our expansion into those markets. As part of those development plans, you will be required to develop roadmaps for the entitlement and interconnection processes that will allow for optimal development in nascent markets. You will be expected to identify potential development spending in new markets and work closely with our valuation and pricing teams to develop a hypothesis on the deal economics and return on investment for the vertical. You will also be expected to begin project development activities on dozens of sites, negotiating agreements with landowners, starting the entitlement and interconnection processes, and moving projects through New Leaf Energy's project development process with a considerable expectation of autonomy and strategic vision.

On the DG Capacity team, the New Markets Project Developer reports to the Vice President of DG Capacity and partners with the Senior Director of Development to execute campaigns. The role secures site control, applies for interconnection, and manages initial site discovery, taking projects through Kickoff before handing them off to Regional Teams.

Position responsibilities will include, but are not limited to:

New Market Development

• Create market entry strategies for new markets based on market size, market maturity, and ability to overcome critical path items of a market to achieve desired target market share;

• Identify critical path items at the market and project level in a new market and establish solutions for resolution;

• Translate development requirements into a set of sequential timely development activities from site control to Membership Interest Purchase and Sale Agreements (MIPA);

• Establish development budgets and spending waterfalls (i.e. thresholds of development) based on de-risking of market-level and/or project-level critical path items;

• Identify within each pillar of development (real estate, interconnection, design, permitting, valuation) the critical path items for project development to take a site from Letter of Intent (LOI) to MIPA closing;

• Work with consultants to identify the required steps to permitting a project in a new market;

• Set up prospecting infrastructure in new markets;

• Maintain accurate information about verticals in New Leaf Energy's systems of record.

DG Capacity Campaign Execution

• Execute the prospecting campaigns designed by the Senior Director of DG Capacity Development, including geography, site criteria, interconnection screens, and landowner and partner outreach;

• Apply the team's relative-value screens when advancing sites: energy communities, favorable AHJs, efficient DG interconnection, and the same utility zone as large loads;

• Identify and research leads through targeted land campaign work, including mailers, phone calls, and other outreach;

• Secure site control, submit interconnection applications, and manage initial site discovery and vendors, including:

• Real estate negotiations: with support from senior team members, manage landowner relationships and negotiate key real estate agreements, including leases, easements, and purchase agreements, to assemble all necessary land for the project;

• Site due diligence and permitting: conduct desktop reviews of potential site characteristics, including topography, soil conditions, access, and the likelihood of wetlands and environmental restrictions, among other site conditions; request quotes and release additional on-site work such as surveying and wetland delineations; site walks, and other activities necessary to sufficiently understand the viability of and de-risk a given site.

• Assist in transitioning projects to the appropriate Regional Team at the appropriate time, likely around Kickoff;

• Contribute to the team's goal of signed LOIs and Kickoffs, and track pipeline status and spending against plan.

Project Shaping and Market Building

• Support the Senior Director in shaping projects to grid needs, including sizing, point-of-interconnection selection, and limited or flexible export design;

• Provide project- and site-level inputs to the target-market scorecard, jurisdiction screens, and hosting-capacity analyses;

• Share field intelligence from utilities, AHJs, and landowners with the IX & Market Participation Lead and the Senior Director of Sales, to inform market rules engagement and customer strategy;

• Engage with communities and AHJs, building trust-based relationships on behalf of New Leaf Energy. You will attend local meetings, build connections within host communities, and onboard local permitting and community engagement partners as necessary.

Ways of Working

• Own identified deliverables within specific timeframes, and clearly communicate progress and impacts to a critical path to the VP, Senior Director and project teams;

• Collaborate with civil, design, interconnection engineering, real estate, and legal to move projects forward;

• Contribute to standard work and SOPs for DG Capacity campaigns and the Kickoff handoff, and drive continuous improvement in your work area;

• Demonstrate an understanding of the New Leaf North Star and the three cultural pillars, and practice them.

Desired Qualifications

• Strong knowledge of the solar industry; or, if not, equivalent knowledge and experience in a similar and/or adjacent project development or real estate development context;

• Successful track record in a complex sales or project development environment, preferably in solar and/or energy storage. By "complex sales" we mean large, technical, construction and/or financial products with long sales cycles that require relationship building, lead generation, navigation of complexity, and deal closing;

• Ability to analyze market sizes and develop a competitive landscape to establish target market shares for New Leaf Energy;

• Understanding of market structures and how they translate into financeable development assets (e.g., PPA-backed asset vs. community solar etc.);

• Ability to identify, define & understand the project-level assumptions that drive a project's valuation;

• Experience with distributed generation site control, interconnection applications, and early-stage permitting, preferably for standalone storage or solar-plus-storage projects;

• Familiarity with distribution interconnection and hosting capacity; interest in wholesale capacity markets, PJM, and FERC Order 2222 is a plus;

• Comfort working with GIS tools, site screens, and systems of record such as Salesforce;

• Excellent organizational and time management skills, with the ability to manage many sites at once in a fast-paced environment;

• Ability to work independently in new geographies while collaborating closely with a small, senior team;

• Bachelor's degree or equivalent experience;

• Travel: considerable, at times, depending on where opportunities arise.

Compensation

New Leaf Energy compensates all employees in three ways: market-competitive base salary, plus above-market variable compensation, plus an equity-like program such that all employees experience the benefits of ownership. We use a data-driven and transparent methodology to calibrate compensation that is externally competitive and internally equitable, guided by the New Leaf Energy compensation manual, which is available to all employees.

The base salary for this position is $125,000 annually. Total compensation for this position, however, is driven more by our generous commission plan, which is paid quarterly and about which we can go into detail in the interview process.

Benefits and Culture

New Leaf Energy’s success is driven by our collaborative, long-term relationships based on trust and transparency. We offer a flexible work environment that places significant responsibility in independent teams and creates opportunities for innovation and personal growth. New Leaf Energy’s benefits are designed to appropriately recognize our employees’ contributions and enable a reasonable work/life balance. Our benefits include:

• Competitive salaries and comprehensive benefits, including medical, dental and vision;

• A 401k plan with immediate vesting and a company match (100% of the first three percent of your pay; 50% for the next two percent you contribute);

• An open and self-managed paid time-off policy;

• A hybrid work location policy that supports working from home for part of the week;

• A parental leave policy for both birthing and non-birthing parents, available immediately upon hire;

• Professional development and education assistance.

Commitment to Diversity and Inclusion

New Leaf Energy values the diverse perspectives, skills, experiences, and contributions of all employees. We want all our employees to feel like they belong and are empowered to be their most authentic selves at work. We’re actively committed to fostering diversity and creating an inclusive environment not only within our own organization but also within the clean energy industry.

We seek a diverse candidate pool in this–and every–search. New Leaf Energy provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants without regard to race, color, religion, gender, age, sexual orientation, national origin, ancestry, disability, genetics, veteran status or any other characteristic protected by state, federal and local laws. In addition to federal law requirements, New Leaf Energy complies with applicable state and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment in every location in which the company has facilities. EEO M/F/D/V.

A Note to Third-Party Recruiters

New Leaf Energy’s People + Culture team coordinates all recruiting and hiring at our company. New Leaf Energy does not accept unsolicited resumes from third-party recruiters, staffing firms, or related agencies. Resumes are only accepted from third-party recruiters, staffing firms, or related agencies if a signed agreement is in place AND if external recruiting assistance is authorized by the People + Culture team for a specific position. All unsolicited resumes will be considered the property of New Leaf Energy. New Leaf Energy is not responsible for any fees related to unsolicited resumes.

The primary goal of this role is to create insight, balance, and optimization within the resource pool through effective coordination, planning, and allocation of resources. This enables accurate prioritization and informed decision-making by management.

The position is responsible for planning and managing Technology personnel resources across all projects and work efforts globally. By implementing robust capacity planning and proactive resource identification and allocation, the role aims to increase the likelihood of meeting project schedules and budgets, while minimizing the impact of potential delays.

This is achieved by ensuring that resource demand is consistently aligned with the right quantity, quality, and expertise of available resources, ultimately supporting the successful delivery of all Technology projects and work packages.

Your responsibilities:

• Collaborate with cross functional teams (Centre of Excellence (CoE / manufacturing facility) and the Regions, Markets, contract/client, affiliates and partners where applicable) to support the efficient and effective delivery of products across relevant sites;
• Work closely with Technology Department Heads to assess and define both short- and long-term personnel resource requirements;
• Coordinate with Program Teams to ensure that up-to-date project progress and milestones are accurately reflected in overall resource planning;
• Provide accurate short- to medium-term resource availability data to support the evaluation and acceptance of new work within the Technology department;
• Assign teams and personnel, down to the developer/engineer level, across project-level, work package-level, and individual task-level to help the Project Manager and Project Engineering Coordinator meet key milestones and deadlines;
• Identify potential bottlenecks, conflicts, or resource overloads (e.g., competing resource demands across multiple projects), and proactively address these issues in collaboration with Department Heads and Project Leads;
• Propose and implement solutions to optimize resource allocation across multiple projects, ensuring alignment with critical path requirements and overall priorities.
• Coordinate personnel resources and match supply with demand for various projects and work packages on a daily, weekly, and monthly basis;
• Maintain an up-to-date, accessible resource management tool that provides visibility across the management team and stakeholders for data-driven decision-making.
• Monitor technical skillsets and capabilities within the Technology department, identifying and reporting potential resource gaps to management;
• Drive continuous improvement of tools and processes for labour forecasting and resource allocation;
• Act as the Technology coordinator for the provision and management of contractor personnel.
• Provide accurate reporting on personnel levels in relation to current and future requirements, ensuring alignment with Performance Indicators and thresholds;
• Assist Project Managers with identifying labour underspends or overspends, recommending corrective actions to stay on schedule and within budget;
• Oversee the Earned Value Management (EVM) process ensuring accurate monitoring and reporting of Technology programs and projects.

• Proven, relevant experience in resource planning, capacity management, or project control, preferably within a technical or project-driven environment.
• Bachelor’s degree (or equivalent) in Business Administration, Project Management, Industrial Engineering, or a related field.
• Excellent analytical skills with the ability to interpret and manage complex planning data and translate insights into actionable solutions.
• Highly proficient in Microsoft Excel (advanced formulas, pivot tables, data analysis); experience with resource management or planning tools is an advantage.
• Strong communication and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively across multiple departments and stakeholder levels.
• Proactive, structured, and detail-oriented approach, with strong organizational and prioritization abilities.
• Fluent in English (written and spoken).
• Ability to perform well under pressure and maintain oversight in a dynamic, international environment.

What we offer:

• A full-time position in a dynamic, international environment with colleagues from around the world;
• An attractive salary based on experience and knowledge;
• Opportunities for training and professional development, supporting both personal growth and career advancement.
• A range of additional benefits, including fresh fruit at the office, a bicycle lease scheme, a performance-based bonus package, corporate fitness program, commuting allowance, and a home working allowance.

We believe that different perspectives and backgrounds are what make a company flourish. All qualified applicants will receive equal consideration for employment regardless of color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, economic status, disability, age, or any other legally protected characteristics. We are proud to be an inclusive company with values grounded in equality and ethics, where we celebrate, support, and embrace diversity.

At no time during the hiring process will Smiths Detection, nor any of our recruitment partners ever request payment to enable participation – including, but not limited to, interviews or testing. Avoid fraudulent requests by applying jobs directly through our career’s website.
